description:
I postprocess SVG files created by svglite with `inkscape --export-text-to-path` to not depend on fonts which may only be installed locally. This converts `<text>` elements into `<path>` elements, at which point the `.svglite path` style sets in, which renders the text with `fill: none;
stroke: #000000;`, which makes text completely illegible.

Is the `<style>` element necessary? Could there be an option to omit it?

Recently, it seems that GGPLOT SVGLITE output cannot be read downstream by Python REPORTLAB via SVGLIB SVG2RLG. 

I get this error:

'lxml.etree._Element' object has no attribute 'ancestors'

I have SVG images from other sources that reimport into REPORTLAB fine.

I find that if I fiddle with the SVG file produced by SVGLITE by removing the first defs, all works as it should.

REMOVE THIS FROM SVGLITE GENERATED FILE

`<defs>
  <style type='text/css'><![CDATA[
    .svglite line, .svglite polyline, .svglite polygon, .svglite path, .svglite rect, .svglite circle {
      fill: none;
      stroke: #000000;
      stroke-linecap: round;
      stroke-linejoin: round;
      stroke-miterlimit: 10.00;
    }
  ]]></style>
</defs>`

I have included the SVGLITE generated file.

![myfile](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/45264234/164327145-29ef06c4-eee6-41f5-a5de-0e6eba6c898d.svg)

I do not want to blame SVGLITE because it may be the downstream Python library that reads it.

Any help is appreciated.

Lou

Was getting overlapping text in a pretty simple legend from ggplot:

![image](https://cloud.githubusercontent.com/assets/22533773/18977829/3384661c-8701-11e6-8707-4fd65f76cd8f.png)

After a bit of playing with the svg I found if you replaced the pt sizes with px instead it produced correctly sized text.

So just made the two following changes to get the output I was expecting:

``` C++
double svg_strwidth(const char *str, const pGEcontext gc, pDevDesc dd) {
  // ... the rest
  return fm.width;//formerly * 96 / 72;
}

inline void write_style_fontsize(SvgStreamPtr stream, double value, bool first = false) {
  if(!first)  (*stream) << ' ';
  (*stream) << "font-size: " << value << "px;"; //formerly pt
}
```

Probably inadvisable, but I found this useful to leverage existing `lattice`/`ggplot2` examples as tests quickly and easily by overriding the `print` methods.

``` r
print.ggplot <- function(x){
  htmlSVG(
    ggplot2:::print.ggplot(x)
  )
}

print.trellis <- function(x){
  htmlSVG(
    lattice:::print.trellis(x)
  )
}
```

For instance, I added in `common.R` from your `ggplot-book` with `results='asis'` and tested all the sections within a couple minutes.  Or, I `demo("lattice")` to do the whole set.
